from review import card,save,DATE
i='TCS-3635';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Theorem 3, Section 3.2 definitions of approximation schemes, and Section 5: remove the fan-class assumption.'
refs += [dict(id='journal',title='Approximate Counting CSP Seen from the Other Side',authors='Andrei A. Bulatov; Stanislav Živný',year=2020,url='https://doi.org/10.1145/3389390',pdf_url='https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/Stanislav.Zivny/publications/bz20toct-preprint.pdf',locator='TOCT 12(2), 2020; author version accepted January 2020, Theorems 3.1–3.2, Sections 2 and 3.2, and Section 5. Theorem 3.2 is the journal numbering of conference Theorem 3.'),dict(id='list',title='Counting List Homomorphisms from Graphs of Bounded Treewidth: Tight Complexity Bounds',authors='Jacob Focke; Dániel Marx; Paweł Rzążewski',year=2024,url='https://doi.org/10.1145/3640814',locator='TALG 20(2), article 11, published 13 February 2024; fixed target graph and exact counting with source treewidth as parameter, a different classification.')]
notes=['Expanded Theorem 3 into the unrestricted bounded-arity approximation dichotomy, retaining recursive enumerability and the randomized parameterized complexity assumption.','Defined both input structures, explicit relational encoding, homomorphisms, raw Gaifman treewidth and left-structure parameter.','Specified multiplicative accuracy, zero counts, uniform scheme time, and all four equivalent conditions.','Checked the 2020 journal version and later fixed-target counting results without confusing decision, exact counting and approximate counting.']
save(i,dict(title='Treewidth classification of approximate counting CSP',formal='Assume that parameterized Clique has no randomized fixed-parameter algorithm with bounded error. For every fixed recursively enumerable class C of finite relational structures with a uniform bound on relation arity, are the following equivalent: (1) counting homomorphisms A→B for A∈C and arbitrary compatible B is exactly solvable in polynomial time; (2) this problem admits a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme; (3) it admits a fixed-parameter randomized approximation scheme with parameter ‖A‖; and (4) the Gaifman graphs of structures in C have bounded treewidth? The missing implication is (3)⇒(4). No fan-grid containment, minor closure or other closure property is assumed for C. This is the source’s Theorem 3 with its fan-class hypothesis removed.',definitions='A finite relational signature τ is a finite collection of relation symbols, each with a specified nonnegative integer arity. A finite τ-structure A consists of a finite universe V(A) and, for every r-ary R∈τ, a set Rᴬ⊆V(A)ʳ. Input structures A and B share τ, but τ may vary between instances. Every arity is at most a constant r_C fixed for the class C. Universes, symbols, arities and relation tuples are explicitly listed in a standard binary encoding; ‖A‖ denotes its length, and similarly for B. Nullary relations, if present, are either empty or contain the empty tuple and are checked in the same way as other relations. A homomorphism is any function h:V(A)→V(B) such that for each relation tuple (a₁,…,aᵣ)∈Rᴬ, the tuple (h(a₁),…,h(aᵣ)) belongs to Rᴮ. It need not be injective or surjective. Count all such functions, including zero when none exists. C is fixed before the input and can be enumerated by a Turing machine; a promised input has A∈C, and an algorithm need not decide membership in C. The Gaifman graph G(A) has universe V(A) as vertices and joins distinct elements occurring together in some relation tuple. A tree decomposition assigns vertex bags to a tree so that every vertex is in a bag, every edge has both ends in a bag, and bags containing a given vertex form a connected subtree. Its width is the largest bag size minus one; treewidth is the minimum width, with empty graph assigned width zero here. Bounded treewidth of C means one constant w works for every A∈C. It refers to A itself, not to a homomorphically equivalent core. An approximation scheme receives A,B and a rational 0<ε<1 and outputs an integer random variable X satisfying Pr[|X−H|≤εH]≥3/4, where H is the homomorphism count. In particular it must output zero with that probability when H=0. Probability is over internal fair random bits. An FPRAS uses time polynomial in ‖A‖+‖B‖+ε⁻¹; an FPTRAS uses f(‖A‖)·(‖A‖+‖B‖+ε⁻¹)^a for a computable f and constant a. Reading the rational accuracy encoding is included. These are worst-case time bounds, and a,f and the algorithm may depend on fixed C but not on individual input structures. The hypothesis means that no uniform randomized algorithm decides whether an N-vertex graph has a k-clique in g(k)N^b time for computable g and constant b, with error at most 1/4 on every instance. This states explicitly the randomized parameterized separation used in the source.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Under the stated parameterized complexity hypothesis, prove the equivalence for every eligible class C, or refute it by a recursively enumerable bounded-arity class of unbounded Gaifman treewidth with an FPTRAS for its homomorphism-counting problem. The other implications are already known. An exact-counting hardness result, a theorem only for minor-closed or fan classes, or a classification with the right-hand structure fixed does not establish the missing unrestricted implication.',context_blocks=[dict(title='The parameterized approximation direction is the obstacle',text='Bounded treewidth gives exact polynomial-time counting, which yields an FPRAS and then an FPTRAS. The source proves the converse under its additional fan-class hypothesis; its conclusion asks whether that hypothesis can be removed.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Exact counting and decision have different structural boundaries',text='The journal discusses the exact-counting classification by treewidth of the structures themselves. Decision can instead depend on treewidth modulo homomorphic equivalence. Replacing raw treewidth by core treewidth would change this approximation question.',citation='journal'),dict(title='The journal version retains the unrestricted question',text='Theorem 3.2 keeps the fan-class condition, including the minor-closed cases, and Section 5 repeats the question for all bounded-arity classes. The indexing change from conference Theorem 3 does not signal a broader theorem.',citation='journal'),dict(title='A later fine-grained classification fixes the other side',text='The 2024 list-homomorphism article gives exact running-time bases for each fixed target graph H, using treewidth of the source graph as a parameter. Here C restricts the source structures and the entire target structure B is unrestricted input.',citation='list')],why='Would determine whether approximation enlarges the tractable classes of structurally restricted counting CSPs, and whether treewidth remains the complete structural criterion.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2019',text='The MFCS paper proves the approximation dichotomy for fan classes and asks to extend it to all recursively enumerable bounded-arity classes.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2020',text='The journal version retains the additional condition and the general open question.',citation='journal'),dict(date='2024-02-13',text='The checked later list-homomorphism classification addresses fixed targets and exact counting, rather than this unrestricted approximation implication.',citation='list')],status='open',status_note='Read the full conference and journal definitions, assumptions, theorem statements and concluding questions. Searches through 12 September 2026 did not locate an unrestricted extension or a counterexample. The later fixed-target exact-counting result has a different input restriction and resource parameter. Search is not exhaustive.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='Recovered the complete conditional equivalence and made the missing FPTRAS-to-bounded-treewidth implication explicit without retaining the fan-class restriction.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A general structural tractability classification for approximate counting of constraint solutions. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['The input asks how many homomorphisms map a source structure A from a fixed class C into an arbitrary target structure B.','The conjecture says a fixed-parameter randomized approximation scheme exists exactly when C has bounded Gaifman treewidth.','Relation arity is uniformly bounded, C is recursively enumerable, and the parameter is the size of A.','The source proves this under a further fan-class condition and asks to remove it.','The 2020 journal version retains that restriction, so exact-counting and fixed-target classifications do not settle the missing approximation direction.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','journal','list'])),notes=notes,checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.07922'])
